# smp-parallel - two harts on one crystal, sharing one RAM, running at once.
#
# The fixture for `ThreadingMode::Parallel` on a board shaped like the ones
# that ship. `machines/tests/heterogeneous.machine` is the *other* parallel
# fixture and answers a different question: two cores of different
# architectures on two separate crystals, which is the configuration the clock
# model was designed for. This one is the configuration real SMP hardware has
# and every shipped `-smp` board in `machines/` uses - **one oscillator, two
# processors** - and it exists because that is the case the parallel round has
# to get right and the one nothing was testing.
#
# `docs/techniques/parallel-execution.md` is the argument this file is the
# instrument for. Two things about it are worth stating here.
#
# ## It declares its own threading mode
#
# `threading parallel` is the whole reason this file is not just another
# two-hart board. It is a statement about the *hardware*: these two harts
# execute at the same instant, the way an SMP board's processors do and the way
# a NES's CPU and PPU do not. It is a **default**, not a demand - `rsemu run
# ... --threading deterministic` still wins, and the regression suite relies on
# exactly that - and the worker count is deliberately not sayable here, because
# how many host threads to spend is a property of the run.
#
# **No board in `machines/` proper says this**, and that is deliberate rather
# than an oversight: a state hash is refused outside a deterministic mode, so a
# shipped board that declared `parallel` would take itself out of the
# regression suite for everybody. A user who wants their own copy of
# `riscv-virt-smp` to run its harts concurrently adds one line to it.
#
# ## One crystal and two runnables is a degenerate configuration
#
# A tree has one unit counter and every domain on it is a divider of that
# counter, so advancing either hart's domain advances both.
# `Scheduler::ticks_until_after` says at length why that is outside the clock
# model in *both* threading modes: one counter cannot express one processor
# halted while the other runs. What makes the board work anyway is
# `SchedulerConfig::max_ticks_per_quantum`, which caps what the first runnable
# may take and leaves the rest of the round's span for the second.
#
# It is written here because it is a real limitation of a board every shipped
# `-smp` file has, not a property of this fixture: with a quantum short enough
# that one hart's cap covers the whole round, the second hart gets a budget of
# zero and does not run at all. `docs/techniques/parallel-execution.md` has the
# arithmetic and the test that pins it.
#
# ## Sources
#
# *The RISC-V Instruction Set Manual, Volume I: Unprivileged ISA* for the
# harts; volume II for `mhartid`, which is the only thing that distinguishes
# them. No board vendor's manual is involved - this is a test fixture, not a
# model of anything anyone built.

machine "smp-parallel" {
  # A board whose processors are concurrent, and says so.
  threading parallel

  # How much RAM the two harts share. Everything interesting a litmus test does
  # happens in the first few words of it.
  param shared = 64K

  # One crystal for both harts, exactly as `riscv-virt-smp` has it.
  osc core = 100 MHz

  space mem { width = 64, unassigned = open-bus, endian = "little" }

  # Identical but for `hartid`, which is what `mhartid` reports and what a
  # guest program uses to decide which half of the fixture it is.
  object cpu0 "cpu.riscv" {
    clock  = core
    space  = mem
    engine = "interp"
    xlen   = "rv64"
    isa    = "imac"
    hartid = 0
    reset  = 0x1000
  }

  object cpu1 "cpu.riscv" {
    clock  = core
    space  = mem
    engine = "interp"
    xlen   = "rv64"
    isa    = "imac"
    hartid = 1
    reset  = 0x1000
  }

  # `image` names a media slot: whoever runs the machine binds the bytes, and
  # nothing below host/ opens a file. Both harts come out of reset in the same
  # ROM and branch on `mhartid`.
  object rom "rom" { size = 4K, image = "code" }
  object ram "ram" { size = shared }

  map mem 0x00001000 size 4K     = rom
  map mem 0x00100000 size shared = ram
}
